## 4. Fixed-reference observable behavior oracle

All source citations in this section refer to reference commit `50373c7b0ff58078727af2c2aac70e29d5e74752`.

### 4.1 Modes, spaces, components, and attachment

- Translate, rotate, and scale geometry is shown together; the clicked component selects the operation (`src/gizmo_transform_vk.hpp:37-39`).
- The only supported transform space is world; local space is explicitly commented as unsupported (`src/gizmo_transform_vk.hpp:55-59`). MGIF must not add a visible Local mode as part of parity P7.
- Components are translate X/Y/Z and XY/XZ/YZ, rotate X/Y/Z, scale X/Y/Z, and uniform scale (`src/gizmo_transform_vk.hpp:61-80`). `eRotateScreen` exists in the enum but is filtered out of rendering (`src/gizmo_transform_vk.cpp:1079-1103`); it is not a P7 component.
- The attachment is node-backed. Only Node and Primitive selections route to a node attachment; the parent world matrix is supplied separately (`src/renderer.cpp:903-950`). No selection means `clearAttachment()` (`src/renderer.cpp:926-933`).
- `clearAttachment()` directly clears attached pointers, hover, active drag, and component state without calling the transform-end callback (`src/gizmo_transform_vk.cpp:184-194`). This is why detach is observably distinct from release.

### 4.2 Handle geometry and visual constants

The reference defaults are a behavioral baseline, not code to copy:

| Element | Fixed-reference value | Evidence |
|---|---:|---|
| Nominal gizmo size | 50 logical pixels | `src/gizmo_transform_vk.hpp:108-110` |
| Axis shaft | radius 0.02, length 1.0 | `src/gizmo_transform_vk.hpp:130-132` |
| Translation cone | radius 0.06, height 0.25, 16 segments | `src/gizmo_transform_vk.hpp:133-135` |
| Rotation ring | radius 1.3, tube radius 0.020, 64 x 16 segments | `src/gizmo_transform_vk.hpp:137-141` |
| Scale cube | size 0.15, axis length 1.67 | `src/gizmo_transform_vk.hpp:143-146` |
| Uniform center box | 2.0 times the scale cube | `src/gizmo_transform_vk.hpp:143-146` |
| Translation plane | size 0.22, offset 0.5, alpha 0.45 | `src/gizmo_transform_vk.hpp:148-151` |
| Axis palette | X red, Y green, Z blue semantic families | `src/gizmo_transform_vk.hpp:112-120` |
| Occlusion dither default | false | `src/gizmo_transform_vk.hpp:153-154` |

The reference's plane alpha is implemented as checkerboard discard, then opaque output, not conventional alpha blending (`shaders/gizmo_visuals.slang:104-114`). MGIF visual baselines must distinguish that appearance from a blended translucent quad.

### 4.3 Ray construction and hit testing

- Mouse coordinates are unprojected with zero-to-one depth using near NDC z=0 and far NDC z=1 (`src/gizmo_transform_vk.cpp:868-897`). The reference describes Y as top-to-bottom and uses the incoming viewport-local mouse convention.
- The ray is transformed once into gizmo-local space, then every visible component first uses an AABB broad phase (`src/gizmo_transform_vk.cpp:568-596`).
- Axis, axis-scale, and uniform boxes use a 0.6 local-distance threshold; translate planes and rotation rings use 0.2 (`src/gizmo_transform_vk.cpp:598-638`).
- Ring selection intersects the ring plane and measures radial distance. Plane intersection rejects `abs(dot(ray, normal)) < 1e-6` and negative t (`src/gizmo_transform_vk.cpp:1038-1049`).
- The closest qualifying component wins (`src/gizmo_transform_vk.cpp:634-638`). Tie behavior and iteration order should be frozen by MGIF unit tests because multiple handles overlap near the origin.
- MGIF must use its `ProjectionConvention` rather than hard-code Vulkan Y/depth assumptions. MGIF Vulkan, D3D12, and Metal all use reverse-Z, while Vulkan alone flips Y (`render/ClipSpaceConvention.h:21-45`).

### 4.4 Drag math

- Axis translation builds a best-view plane from the axis and view direction, intersects the current ray, and projects the hit delta onto the axis (`src/gizmo_transform_vk.cpp:668-683`, `723-746`).
- Plane translation intersects the component plane and applies the world delta converted to local parent space (`src/gizmo_transform_vk.cpp:686-697`, `750-768`).
- Rotation intersects the axis-normal plane, compares start/current vectors, and applies a signed angular delta (`src/gizmo_transform_vk.cpp:772-818`).
- Scale uses total screen delta, `1 + (dx - dy) * 0.005`, clamped to at least 0.01 (`src/gizmo_transform_vk.cpp:858-865`). This is the reference drag response, even though R06 separately requires legal authored zero/negative scale from numeric editing.
- Begin snapshots the initial TRS, Preview mutates the node, and End constructs/pushes a transform command (`src/renderer.cpp:363-391`). Preview must therefore be a transaction, not a per-frame history push.

### 4.5 Parent-space transform behavior

- Display position is parent-world multiplied by local position (`src/gizmo_transform_vk.cpp:963-990`).
- Gizmo axes come from normalized parent matrix columns; the selected node's own rotation does not orient the handles in the supported world mode (`src/gizmo_transform_vk.cpp:963-971`).
- A world movement is converted with the inverse parent matrix and a direction w=0 (`src/gizmo_transform_vk.cpp:998-1009`).
- Negative and non-uniform parent scales can reflect/skew the effective basis or make inversion ill-conditioned. The source is not proof of robust behavior for those cases; E018/V03 require deterministic finite handling and visual baselines.
- MGIF should compute ray/drag intermediates in double precision, validate determinant/condition and finiteness, and fail closed before preview mutation if the parent transform cannot yield a stable result. A disabled attachment in such a case is allowed only if it follows the locked K06 failure policy and is visibly explained; it is not a general parity waiver.

### 4.6 Snapping

- Scalar snap is nearest multiple: `round(value / step) * step` (`src/gizmo_transform_vk.cpp:47-52`).
- Axis and plane translation snap the complete resulting local position vector, including components not moved by the current handle (`src/gizmo_transform_vk.cpp:740-764`).
- Rotation snaps the angular delta, and scale snaps resulting affected component(s).
- Runtime defaults are Grid/translation 1.0, rotation 45 degrees, scale 0.1; Grid/Gizmo/Snap start disabled (`src/resources.hpp:129-140`).
- The reference UI ranges are Grid Unit `[0.001,1000]`, rotation `[1,180]` degrees, and scale `[0.001,10]` (`src/ui_renderer.cpp:460-473`).
- Grid Unit feeds both the grid base unit and translation snap (`src/renderer.cpp:866-875`).

### 4.7 Constant pixel scale, hover, and active state

- The source formula is `(sizePixels / viewportHeight) * clipW` (`src/gizmo_transform_vk.cpp:1028-1036`). It is evidence of intent for perspective projection, not an adequate cross-projection algorithm.
- Orthographic scale, behind-camera clip W, very small viewport height, resize-frame mismatch, and DPI are not closed by source evidence. MGIF must derive world-units-per-logical-pixel from projection type and the immutable viewport snapshot, and validate the projected result.
- Rendering explicitly colors by `m_hoveredComponent` (`src/gizmo_transform_vk.cpp:320-334`), and hover is updated only when the left button is not down (`src/gizmo_transform_vk.cpp:255-258`). There is no independent reference active-color token in this code. During a normal drag, the pre-press hover usually remains highlighted.
- Consequently, P7 must capture normal/hover/active reference images before defining an MGIF active token. It must not invent a visually distinct active state and then call it parity.

### 4.8 Input capture and event ordering

- Release is processed before drag update and press; a delivered release ends an active drag once (`src/gizmo_transform_vk.cpp:221-260`).
- Viewport processing continues while the gizmo is dragging even if the viewport is no longer hovered (`src/ui_renderer.cpp:52-58`). Gizmo processing precedes scene picking, and an active/dragging gizmo returns before picking (`src/ui_renderer.cpp:68-89`).
- No explicit OS pointer-capture call was found in the fixed reference. The observable behavior is logical capture plus continued polling. MGIF should own the capture adapter and test OS/window edges rather than expose GLFW/Win32 types from editor code.
- Escape is a global selection clear, not a gizmo rollback (`src/ui_renderer.cpp:879-883`). There is no visible cancel API.

The authoritative target state table is:

| Input branch | Selection | Preview TRS | Attachment/drag | Transform history |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Normal delivered release | Retained | Final preview retained | End once, release capture | Exactly one command |
| Release outside viewport after inside press | Retained | Final preview retained | End once, release capture | Exactly one command |
| O05 Escape-only while down | Cleared | Latest preview retained | Detach without End | Unchanged; late release is a no-op |
| O06 Escape + release in one input batch, either order, no rendered frame between | Cleared | Latest preview retained | Detach/resolved inactive | **Unchanged; Undo remains disabled** |
| O07 focus loss, no delivered release | Retained until some later clear | Preview retained | Reference drag remains latched; later clear detaches | Unchanged |
| O07 mouse-up then focus loss in same input queue but no app-visible release | Retained | Preview retained | Remains latched | Unchanged |
| O07 release delivered in preceding app frame, then focus loss | Retained | Final preview retained | Already ended | Exactly one command; focus loss adds none |
| Repeated Escape, second release, or late release after detach/end | No further change | No further change | Inactive | No additional command |

O05 details are supported by `manifest.json:320-350`; O06 by `manifest.json:353-389`; O07 by `manifest.json:392-423`. The executable probe's exact focus partitions are recorded at `INPUT-CLIPBOARD-REPORT.md:84-92`. E040/E072/E081 make the branches mutually exclusive and idempotent.

### 4.9 Grid and overlay composition

- The grid is a procedural XZ-plane visual overlay, not a pickable object (`shaders/gizmo_grid.slang:20-29`).
- It draws three adjacent LOD levels, two axes, and `numLines` lines per level; the default is 151 lines, base unit 1.0 (`src/gizmo_grid_vk.hpp:82-84`; `shaders/gizmo_grid.slang:23-25`).
- Four decade steps are generated from base unit. Perspective LOD blends floor-ray distance and camera height; orthographic LOD uses height (`src/gizmo_grid_vk.cpp:119-149`, `164-184`).
- Each level snaps its center to the active step (`shaders/gizmo_grid.slang:80-104`), suppresses coincident coarser lines and X/Z-axis duplicates (`shaders/gizmo_grid.slang:110-130`).
- Grid pixels behind scene depth are discarded and then faded by edge, viewing angle, distance, and per-level alpha (`shaders/gizmo_grid.slang:180-224`). X/Y/Z axes are separate and do not perform the grid's scene-depth discard, so they remain on top.
- The grid uses additive color blending; axes use alpha-over (`src/gizmo_grid_vk.cpp:233-250`, `313-328`). Because MGIF blend state is pipeline-fixed, these require distinct pipelines.
- Reference overlay order is existing tonemapped color (load), grid, then gizmo, before ImGui (`src/gizmo_visuals_vk.cpp:117-176`; `src/renderer.cpp:885-900`).
- A separate helper depth attachment is cleared for the gizmo so gizmo parts self-occlude while scene depth remains sampled (`src/gizmo_visuals_vk.cpp:136-171`, `183-217`).
- Gizmo fragments sample scene depth. With default `occlusionDither=false`, occluded fragments remain fully visible (x-ray); when enabled, occluded fragments use checkerboard discard (`shaders/gizmo_visuals.slang:83-114`).
- The reference patches a perspective projection to extreme near/far before overlay rendering (`src/gizmo_visuals_vk.cpp:33-40`, `165-171`). MGIF must not copy that Vulkan-era helper. It needs an MGIF-owned, projection-convention-aware overlay projection or screen-space extrusion algorithm that is tested for perspective, orthographic, reverse-Z, and all backends.

### 4.10 Scene-root transform is deliberately not gizmo-routed

R13 is adjacent to P7 but has a separate behavior boundary:

- Scene Transform is a popup with numeric Translation/Rotation/Scale and Reset (`src/ui_scene_browser.cpp:521-555`).
- It captures root IDs and ordered baseline local matrices (`src/ui_scene_browser.cpp:589-617`).
- Application computes `sceneTRS * baselineLocal`; decomposable results become TRS, otherwise a matrix remains authoritative (`src/ui_scene_browser.cpp:622-673`).
- A hierarchy change freezes the applied result, resets popup state to identity, and rebuilds baselines on the next application (`src/ui_scene_browser.cpp:559-633`).
- The task locks this route as non-history (`gltf-scene-editor-parity-task.md:450-454`). It must not attach one root, create a group gizmo, or emit a transform command as an implementation shortcut.

